WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.417
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Welcome to "Get

00:00:01.417 --> 00:00:03.660
Cooking in Cloud," where
we share the best recipes

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.730
to apply in your Cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:06.540
I am Priyanka.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.640
And in this episode,
we'll share the recipe

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.790
to plan a hot DR pattern
for your application

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.320
that's deployed on-premise.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.610
So, let's dive in.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.502
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.310
A little recap-- in
the last two episodes,

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:30.870
we talked about Mane Street Art,
that runs their applications

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:34.950
on-premise and are building a DR
infrastructure on Google Cloud.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:39.330
This started with a cold DR plan
and moved to a warm standby,

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:42.570
due to the need for
lower RTO and RPO values.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.260
Well, now, Mane Street Art
has become really popular

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.890
and cannot afford to be
down, even for seconds.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:54.210
Since their requirement is to
achieve a near-zero RTO and RPO

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:58.680
appeal values, the only way
is by running HA architecture

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.710
across their production
environment and Google Cloud

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.180
concurrently.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.360
Let's see how this works
from the setup perspective.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.400
We create a VPC network.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.110
Then we configure
the connectivity

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.860
between the on-premise network
and the Google Cloud network.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.290
We create custom
images of the servers

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.230
in Google Cloud with the
exact same configuration

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.710
as on-premise.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:24.180
Then, configure the replication
between on-premise database

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.000
server and the one
on Google Cloud.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.910
Remember, that if your
database systems permit

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:33.450
only a single rateable database
instance when you configure

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.360
replication, then you
might need to ensure

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.820
that one of the
database replicas act

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.100
as a read-only server.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.170
Create individual
instance templates

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:47.220
that use the images for the
application server and the web

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:48.150
server.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.610
Configure regional
managed instance groups

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.010
for the application
and web servers.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:56.580
Then configure health checks
using Stackdriver monitoring

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.500
and load balancing
using the regional

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.610
managed instance groups.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:04.170
Configure a scheduled a task
to create regular snapshots

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.000
of the persistent disk.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.970
And lastly, configure
the DNS service

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:12.270
to distribute traffic between
your on-premise environment

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.190
and the Google
Cloud environment.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.680
With this hybrid
approach, you need

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:20.070
to use a DNS service that
supports weighted routing

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.170
to the two production
environments

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:25.620
so that you can serve the
same application from both.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:28.110
In case of a failure
on premise, you just

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.500
disable the DNS routing to
the on-premise web server,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.060
and that's it.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.300
In most cases, the DNS
service supports health checks

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.930
and will automatically route all
the traffic to the LD servers

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:41.340
on Google Cloud.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.440
And when it is all
back to normal,

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.480
and can handle
production traffic,

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.000
we need to resynchronize
the databases.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.400
If your database system
doesn't automatically

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.970
promote a read-only replica
to be the rateable primary

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:56.820
on failure, you need
to intervene to ensure

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.310
that the replica is promoted.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.730
After ensuring that, just
enable the DNS routing back

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:06.260
to distribute traffic to both
on-premise and Google Cloud.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.200
Well, there you have it.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.560
If you are running your
application on premise

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.470
and are looking to
achieve very, very small

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.340
RTO and RPO values,
then hopefully you'll

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.920
learn how to approach recovering
the environment from failure

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.060
using Google Cloud hot HA
across the two environments.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.410
That's all for today on
"Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:30.420
Here's hoping you're cooking
up your own DR strategy.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.520
Join us next time
and learn the recipe

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.160
to implement disaster
recovery for applications

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.770
that are built on Google Cloud.

